  "content": "# Merging Overlapping Intervals in Python\n\nHere's a clean implementation with type hints, docstring, and examples:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef merge_intervals(intervals: List[List[int]]) -> List[List[int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping intervals.\n    \n    Given a list of intervals [start, end], returns a new list where\n    any intervals that overlap or touch are combined into a single interval.\n    \n    Args:\n        intervals: A list of [start, end] pairs. Each pair must satisfy\n                   start <= end. The input list may be empty.\n    \n    Returns:\n        A list of merged, non-overlapping intervals sorted by start time.\n    \n    Time complexity:  O(n log n) due to sorting\n    Space complexity: O(n) for the output list\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n    \n    # Sort by start time so we can sweep left-to-right\n    sorted_intervals = sorted(intervals, key=lambda x: x[0])\n    \n    merged: List[List[int]] = [sorted_intervals[0][:]]\n    \n    for start, end in sorted_intervals[1:]:\n        last_start, last_end = merged[-1]\n",
